---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import { siteConfig } from "../site-config";
import SiteChrome from "../components/SiteChrome.astro";

// No hero artwork, on purpose. A course arguing that confident presentation is
// not evidence should not open with a decorative photograph of a lecture
// theatre it does not have.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <SiteChrome />
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    Public guidance is the best summary anyone has of what helps people in
    general, and this course starts from it rather than against it. What it
    cannot do is the last step, from a population to a particular person on a
    particular Tuesday. That step is yours, and it can be taken carefully.
  </p>

  <p>
    The course teaches it in three moves, and the twelve weeks are those moves
    in order. <strong>Realisation</strong>: read the guidance accurately and
    find where your own day sits in it. <strong>Change</strong>: alter one
    thing, using the guidance for what to change and how.
    <strong>Rethinking</strong>: read what your body, your mood and your
    actions send back, and revise against it. Then round again, because you
    will not be the same person in May that you were in March.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will actually do</h2>
  <p>
    Record one ordinary day and change nothing about it. Decide what "tired"
    means precisely enough to write down. Measure one night with two
    instruments and watch them disagree. Take something away and put it back.
    Try to conceal a condition from yourself, and mostly fail. Run the same
    experiment twice, ten weeks apart, and get two different answers.
  </p>

  <p>
    You will hand in a pre-registration before you collect any data, a portfolio
    of protocols, one full report, and one replication. You are marked on the
    quality of your reasoning and never on whether your day improved. See the
    <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>policies</a> for why that distinction is not
    negotiable here.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone, in first year, with no background in statistics or physiology. Every
    technical term is explained where you first meet it and collected in the
    <a href={withBase("/glossary/")}>glossary</a>. If you find writing here that only
    somebody already trained could follow, that is a fault in the course and we
    would like to know.
  </p>

  <p>
    It will not hand you a finished answer, because a finished answer belongs
    to one person across one stretch of days, and it is no substitute for a
    doctor. What it gives you instead is the loop, and the judgement to know
    when a result is worth acting on.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you enrol</h2>
  <p>
    Four of the twelve labs ask you to change something about your sleep, your
    eating or your caffeine, and others ask you to look hard at a record of
    yourself. Every lab above the lowest risk level carries a stopping rule, a
    limit on what the exercise must never become, and an alternative protocol
    that teaches the same method without asking anything of your body. Taking the alternative
    costs no marks and needs no reason. All of it is printed on the week's own
    page, not buried in a handbook.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Schedule" href="/schedule/">
      <p>Every week's lecture, lab and due date in one table.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four pieces, what each is worth, and what is being judged.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Glossary" href="/glossary/">
      <p>Every technical word the course uses, in plain language.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who runs which lab, and who to ask first.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
